From f7d123b77ee4acc11630db255b9005774a8f5129 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vairamani muthusamy <vairamani.muthusamy@emerson.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 14:33:02 +0530
Subject: opkg: add post_invoke transaction hook

Add a "post_invoke" configuration option which names a hook to run after
a successful, state-changing opkg transaction (install, upgrade, remove,
configure or flag).

The value may be a single executable, or a directory of executables that
are run in lexical, run-parts-like order (a "drop-in" directory). This
lets integrators react to package changes -- for example to rebuild an
intrusion-detection baseline or refresh a cache -- without having to wrap
every opkg invocation.

The hook fires once per invocation, from the command-line frontend only,
and is gated on opkg_state_changed so it is not run for "opkg update", for
no-op transactions, for --noaction (dry-run) runs, or when the transaction
fails. libopkg API consumers are unaffected.

Directory entries are selected with scandir()/alphasort(); dotfiles and
non-executable entries are skipped, mirroring standard drop-in directory
semantics. A hook that fails is logged but does not change opkg's exit
status, since the transaction itself has already completed.

Document the new option in opkg.conf.5 and add a core test covering
ordering, executable filtering, and the dry-run/no-op gating.

